      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "hotspotIconType": "icon",
      "hotspotIcon": "plus",
      "hotspotColor": "#a4154b",
      "header": "Panel A — Iron Deficiency Anaemia",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>The \"textbook\" microcytic hypochromic picture. <strong>Microcytosis</strong> (RBCs smaller than a small-lymphocyte nucleus), <strong>hypochromia</strong> (central pallor &gt; ⅓ diameter), <strong>pencil / cigar cells</strong>, and <strong>anisopoikilocytosis</strong>. <strong>HIGH RDW</strong> — a mixed population of new small and older normal cells. This high size-variability is the key contrast with thalassaemia trait.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "cde4649b-727c-43f9-8c25-321c7208ae1f",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Panel A — Iron Deficiency Anaemia"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 75,
        "y": 32,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "hotspotIconType": "icon",
      "hotspotIcon": "plus",
      "hotspotColor": "#a4154b",
      "header": "Panel B — Thalassaemia Trait",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>The great mimicker of IDA — same MCV range, different smear. <strong>Disproportionate microcytosis with LOW / NORMAL RDW</strong>: globin chains are reduced but normal, so all cells are <em>uniformly</em> small. Look for <strong>target cells</strong> and <strong>basophilic stippling</strong>. Uniform small cells + normal RDW ≠ IDA.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "bf0f73dc-baed-4510-981d-63b014a892fe",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Panel B — Thalassaemia Trait"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 25,
        "y": 72,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "hotspotIconType": "icon",
      "hotspotIcon": "plus",
      "hotspotColor": "#a4154b",
      "header": "Panel C — Lead Poisoning",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>A sideroblastic variant with one near-pathognomonic clue: <strong>COARSE basophilic stippling</strong> — large, irregular, numerous deep-blue dots (lead inhibits 5′-nucleotidase → ribosomal-RNA aggregates). Microcytosis is usually mild; a dimorphic population may coexist.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "1bdb8ad0-a5ad-4419-9cb6-9270af4b5504",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Panel C — Lead Poisoning"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "position": {
        "x": 75,
        "y": 72,
        "legacyPositioning": false
      },
      "alwaysFullscreen": false,
      "hotspotIconType": "icon",
      "hotspotIcon": "plus",
      "hotspotColor": "#a4154b",
      "header": "Panel D — Sideroblastic Anaemia",
      "content": [
        {
          "library": "H5P.Text 1.1",
          "params": {
            "text": "<p>Pathognomonic <strong>DIMORPHIC population</strong> — two crowds of cells side by side in one field: small hypochromic (iron-loaded, defective) cells next to normocytic normochromic cells. <strong>Pappenheimer bodies</strong> (peripheral siderotic granules) may be seen.</p>"
          },
          "subContentId": "55e448cf-8db4-44ba-9bb4-e22c3ab6bf89",
          "metadata": {
            "contentType": "Text",
            "license": "U",
            "title": "Panel D — Sideroblastic Anaemia"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
